<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sultan Agung</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1593–<a href="/years/1645">1645</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Sultan Agung of Mataram</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Agung was the third ruler of the Mataram Sultanate in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, reigning from 1613 to 1645. He conquered most of Java's north coast, besieging Surabaya until its fall in 1625 and subduing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumedang">Sumedang</a>, Giri and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a>, and twice besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a>, in 1628 and 1629, without success. He introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javanese-calendar">Javanese calendar</a> that blends Islamic and Saka reckoning.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sultan-agung.jpg" alt="Sultan Agung" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Basuki Bawono · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anyakrakusuma_of_Mataram.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Sultan Agung</b>’s Javanese calendar of 1633 kept the old Saka year count but switched to Islamic lunar months; it is still used for court ceremonies in Java.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Sultan Agung</b> of the Mataram Sultanate conquered Madura in 1624, he installed a local prince, Raden Praseno, as Cakraningrat I to rule the western half of the island from Sampang.</blockquote><a href="/state/bangkalan-kingdom">Bangkalan Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sultan Agung of Mataram</b> conquered the coastal allies one by one and besieged Surabaya for years, finally damming the Brantas to cut off its water.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-surabaya">Duchy of Surabaya</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sultan Agung of Mataram</b> conquered Giri in 1636, using Pangeran Pekik of Surabaya so as not to fight the saints himself.</blockquote><a href="/state/giri-kedaton">Giri Kedaton</a></li><li><blockquote>Squeezed between Banten, Cirebon and the expanding Mataram Sultanate, Sumedang submitted to <b>Sultan Agung of Mataram</b> in 1620, and its ruler became a regent.</blockquote><a href="/state/sumedang-larang">Sumedang Larang</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kotagede">Kotagede</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Died in Kerto, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-7.82,110.4&amp;year=1626" aria-label="Kotagede on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7603 2456 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sultan Agung"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8053 2726h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8053 2726h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-7.82,110.4&amp;year=1626">What was here in 1626? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
